nits:
  - finding: "A stop() racing a restart's spawn() that is awaiting the ready line finds no owned child and returns; spawn then installs the new child without re-checking its generation, so RunEvent::Exit during a Settings restart leaves that child unreaped by the supervisor (verified nit)."
    waived: "The outcome the quit promises still holds: the child runs with --lifeline and its stdin pipe lives only in the app process, so the app's exit gives it EOF and it shuts down and frees its ports (DEC-69 rules 3 and 11). The window is the sub-second wait for a restarted core's ready line, and nobody observes the unpublished state after the app is gone. A generation re-check in spawn is a cheap hardening, not a defect."
  - finding: "Each core log line costs one setEntries, a copy of up to 2000 kept entries in mergeLog, and a re-render of every LogTail row while the Capture service page is open."
    waived: "Bounded by the 2000-line ring and paid only while the page is mounted; the core logs a few lines per start, so a debug-level flood is the only case where it shows, and the page is a diagnostic surface."
  - finding: "The supervisor's stdout/stderr readers use BufReader::lines(), so a single newline-free line from the child is buffered and stored at unbounded length; the ring bounds line count, not bytes."
    waived: "The child is our own core, whose stderr is tracing output line by line; a pathological unterminated line is not an input this process produces."
  - finding: "LOG_LINES = 2000 is declared in both TypeScript and Rust, pinned by a Rust test that reads coreService.ts's source text rather than one value deriving the other."
    waived: "W3's review accepted the shape: the pin fails loudly on drift, and xtask already reads ui/ the same way; a generated constant across the process boundary is more machinery than the one number warrants."
  - finding: "The --core dispatch lives only in the shell, so the trawler binary and the app binary accept different argument grammars, with the shared flags tested on each side."
    waived: "Deliberate by DEC-69 (Q11): --core is the app re-executing itself into the same core entry; the headless binary has no reason to accept a mode switch it already is."
refuted: "TWO CANDIDATES REFUTED. Altitude (important): quitStopsCore re-derives the shell's stop rule - the verifier found it mirrors what the shell actually stops (an owned, live core; Supervisor::stop is a no-op over an exited one, an attached core is never stopped), unit-tested over every state, with J3 pinning that a quit stops the core. Altitude (important): the CoreState/LogEntry wire shapes and command names are unpinned - command names are pinned by both_shell_builds_register_exactly_the_commands_the_app_invokes, the Rust wire shapes by literal-JSON tests in supervisor.rs, and the page end to end by J2 against a real core; the repo's precedent (roadmap: the header-rewrite mirror) accepts hand-mirrored shapes across this boundary."
